{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2109 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a38a2d98b0d43808298dacbaacdb9d135064639e0931f5b174b4159cf33679a6" :edb/db-before-t 2108 :edb/db-after-t 2109 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535421 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:46:29.802Z" 13194139535421 true] [17592186119550 1100 "The matrix $A$ and vectors $\\mathbf{b}_1$, $\\mathbf{b}_2$, $\\mathbf{b}_3$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535421 false] [17592186119550 1100 "The matrix $A$ and vectors $\\mathbf{b}_1$, $\\mathbf{b}_2$, $\\mathbf{b}_3$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\textrm{Null}(A)?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064430/assets/9832b17728be69381a5f534317c0f30156fd32f3706f4cd4634765a02370180e.png)" 13194139535421 true] [17592186119550 1145 "A vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ if\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $\\mathbf{0}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAb_{1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-27 \\\\ 45 \\\\ 72\\end{bmatrix} \\ne 0\\cdot \\\\\nAb_{2} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = 0✓ \\\\\nAb_{3} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix} \\ne 0\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $\\mathbf{b}_2$ lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139535421 false] [17592186119550 1145 "A vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ if $A\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $\\mathbf{0}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-27 \\\\ 45 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\,✓ \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $\\mathbf{b}_2$ lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139535421 true] [17592186119551 1100 "Consider the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064430/assets/a8b13d238a1e77d90abe70a505c68a72060ea95fe545f56ae53e7cf40ba0595e.png) and the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064430/assets/6e9f6c010c3137438923dfc32674dedf6d2d554b9b0a6745b007d826954d1e65.png) Find the value of $k$ such that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139535421 true] [17592186119551 1100 "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the value of $k$ such that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139535421 false] [17592186119551 1145 "If $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A)$, then we must have\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAb &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 + 2k - 12 \\\\ -6 + 3k - 3 \\\\ 12 - 6k + 6\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{2k - 6 = 0; 3k - 9 = 0; - 6k + 18 = 0\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=3$." 13194139535421 false] [17592186119551 1145 "If $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A),$ then we must have $A\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{0}.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6+2k-12 \\\\ -6+3k-3 \\\\ 12-6k+6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2k-6=0 \\\\ 3k-9=0 \\\\ -6k+18=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=3.$" 13194139535421 true] [17592186119552 1100 "Consider the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064430/assets/787a2e53b8e39e79e0e4a2379082187a445a9c6babe0677fa315c516c01f0f67.png) Find $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139535421 true] [17592186119552 1100 "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ Find $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139535421 false] [17592186119552 1145 "We need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} &:=\\frac{1}{4}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} - 3x_{2} = 0; x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that $x_2=0$. Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} - 3x_{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx_{1} - 3(0) &= 0 \\\\\nx_{1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\left\\{\\mathbf{0} \\right\\}\n$$" 13194139535421 false] [17592186119552 1145 "We need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3(0) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064430/assets/f73d7951a041c633d78d1d6971a3473800727452bb92e1b39ad574a045e1b76f.png) is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\left\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.$" 13194139535421 true] [17592186119736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348349 13194139535421 true] [17592186119736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348356 13194139535421 true] [17592186119736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348390 13194139535421 true] [17592186119736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348397 13194139535421 true] [17592186119736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348417 13194139535421 true] [17592186119737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348363 13194139535421 true] [17592186119737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348370 13194139535421 true] [17592186119737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348404 13194139535421 true] [17592186119737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348424 13194139535421 true] [17592186119738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348383 13194139535421 true] [17592186119738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348431 13194139535421 true] [17592186119738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348438 13194139535421 true] [17592186120257 1017 #edb/ref 17592186118042 13194139535421 false] [17592186120257 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348377 13194139535421 true] [17592186120257 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535421 true] [17592186120257 1100 "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $Null(A)$." 13194139535421 false] [17592186120257 1100 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139535421 true] [17592186120257 1145 "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have one free variable, ${x}_{2}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2},$ so the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}.\n$$" 13194139535421 true] [17592186120298 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535421 true] [17592186120298 1100 "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -15 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $b = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$. Find the value of $k$ such that $b ∈ Null(A)$." 13194139535421 false] [17592186120298 1100 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -15 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139535421 true] [17592186120298 1145 "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -15 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3k-15 \\\\ -2+k-3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3k-15=0 \\\\ k-5=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=5.$" 13194139535421 true] [17592186120299 1017 #edb/ref 17592186118269 13194139535421 false] [17592186120299 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348411 13194139535421 true] [17592186120299 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535421 true] [17592186120299 1100 "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $Null(A)$." 13194139535421 false] [17592186120299 1100 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139535421 true] [17592186120299 1145 "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have two free variables, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}$  and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3},$ so the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}.\n$$" 13194139535421 true] [17592186348349 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348350 13194139535421 true] [17592186348349 1089 "q-111079" 13194139535421 true] [17592186348349 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535421 true] [17592186348349 1100 "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535421 true] [17592186348349 1113 #uuid "14aa32cf-8550-54fc-b750-a589ef676fd8" 13194139535421 true] [17592186348349 1145 "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\,✓ \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{3}$ lie in $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139535421 true] [17592186348350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348351 131